","a. **Series System**  
In a series system, the system fails if one or more components fail. For the system to be reliable, all components must be reliable.  
- The reliability function \( R_S(t) \) for a series system is the product of the reliability functions of all components:  
  \[
  R_S(t) = R_1(t) \times R_2(t) \times R_3(t) \times R_4 \times R_5 \times R_6 \times R_7 \times R_8 \times R_9 \times R_{10} \times R_{11} \times R_{11} \times R_{13} \times R_{14} \times R_{15} \times R_{16} \times R_{17} \times R_{18} \times R_{19} \times R_{20} \times R_{21} \times R_{22} \times R_{23} \times R_{24} \times R_{25} \times R_{26} \times R_{27} \cdots R_{n}(t)
  \]  
  where \( R_i(t) \) is the reliability function of component \( i \).  

b. **Parallel System**  
In a parallel system, the system is ready if one or more components are reliable. The system fails only if all components fail.  
- The reliability function \( R_S(t) \) for a parallel system is the complement of the probability that all cells fail:  
  \[
  R_S(t) = 1 - \left(1 - R_1(t)\right) \times (1 - R_2(t)) \times (1 - R_3(t)) \times (1 - R_4) \times (1 - R_5) \times (1 - R_6} \times (1 - R_7) \times (1 - R_8) \times (1 - R_9) \times (1 - R_{10}) \times (1 - R_{11}) \times (1 - R_{11}) \times (1 - R_{13}) \times (1 - R_{14}) \times (1 - R_{15}) \times (1 - R_{16}) \times (1 - R_{17}) \times (1 - R_{18}) \times (1 - R_{19}) \times (1 - R_{20}) \times (1 - R_{21}) \times (1 - R_{22}) \times (1 - R_{23}) \times (1 - R_{24}) \times (1 - R_{25}) \times (1 - R_{26}) \times (1 - R_{27}) \times (1 - r_{n}(t))
  \]  
  where \( R_i(t) \) is the reliability function of component \( i \).  

c) **Combination of Series and Parallel Systems**  
For a combination of series and parallel systems, the reliability function is derived by combining the results of the series and parallel components.  

- **Series-Parallel Combination**:  
