   The reaction between sulfur and oxygen is:
   \[
   \text{S(s) + 2\text{O}_2(g) \rightarrow \text{SO}_2(g)
   \]
   This shows that 1 mole of sulfur reacts with 2 moles of oxygen.

4. **Moles of sulfur required:**
   \[
   \text{Moles of sulfur} = \frac{\text{Moles of oxygen}}{2} = \frac{1.95 \text{ mol}}{2} = 0.975 \text{ mol}
   \]

5. **Mass of sulfur required:**
   \[
   \text{Mass of sulfur} = \text{Moles of sulfur} \times \text{Molar mass of sulfur} = 0.975 \text{ mol} \times 32 \text{ g/mol} = 31.2 \text{ g}
   \]

