The bond between nitrogen and oxygen in the molecule NO2 is **polar**. The polarity arises because the electronegativity difference between nitrogen and oxygen is significant. Oxygen is more electronegative (17.7) than nitrogen (16.0). This means that oxygen attracts electrons in the bond more electronegative, resulting in a partial negative charge (δ-) on the oxygen and a positive charge (δ+) on the nitrogen. Thus, the bond has a dipole moment, making it polar.
