   \lim_{t \to \infty} \left[ \frac{1}{3} \sin t + \cos t \right) e^{-t/2}.
   \]
   Since \( \sin t \) and \( \cos t \) oscillate between -1 and 1, the expression \( \frac{1}{3} \sin t + \cos t \) oscillates between \( \frac{1}{3} - 1 = -\frac{2}{3} \) and \( 1 + \frac{1}{3} = \frac{4}{3} \). Thus, the function \( \frac{1}{3} \sin t + \cos t \) is bounded in the interval \( \left[ -\frac{2}{3}, \frac{4}{3} \right] \).

   The exponential term \( e^{-t/2} \) approaches 0 as \( t \to \infty \). Thus, the product of the oscillating function and the exponential function approaches 0.

   \[
