","The magnetic field strength \( B \) at the center of a circular coil is given by the formula:

\[
B = \frac{\mu_0 N I}{2r}
\]

Where:
- \( B \) is the magnetic field strength (in Tesla)
- \( \mu_0 \) is the permeability of free space, approximately \( 4\pi \times 10^{-7} \, \text{T m/A} \).
- \( N \) is the number of turns in the coil.
- \( I \) is the current (in Amperes).
- \( r \) is the radius of the coil (in meters).

Substituting the given values:
- \( N = 100 \)
- \( I = 1 \, \text{A} \)
- \( r = 0.2 \, \text{m} \)

\[
B = \frac{(4\pi \times 10^{-7} \, \text{T m/A}) \times 100 \times 1}{2 \times 0.2 \, \text{m}}
\]

\[
B = \frac{4\pi \times 10^{-5} \, \text{T m}}{0.4 \, \text{m}}
\]

\[
B = 10 \pi \times 10^{-5} \, \text{T}
\]

\[
B = 3.14 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{T}
\]

Thus, the magnetic field strength at the center of the coil is \( \boxed{3.14 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{T}} \).",fluency,"# Task Description
