An amplifier has a voltage gain \( A_v = 100 \) without feedback. A fraction \( m_v = 0.1 \) of the output is fed back in opposition to the input (negative feedback). If the gain \( A_v \) falls by 6 dB due to ageing, calculate the percentage change in the closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \).
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
1. **Calculate the initial closed-loop gain \( A_{fv} \):**
   The negative feedback gain is given by:
   \[
   A_{fv} = \frac{A_v}{1 + m_v}
   \]
   Substituting the given values:
   \[
   A_{fv} = \frac{100}{1 + 0.1} = \frac{100}{1.1} = 90.9 \text{ V/V}
   \]

2. **Determine the new voltage gain \( A_v' \):**
   The gain decreases by 6 dB, so the new gain is:
   \[
   A_{vf} = 10^{-0.1 \times 6} \times A_v = 1.11 \times 100 = 111 \text{ V/V}
   \]

3. **Calculate the new closed-loop gain \( A_{fv}' \):**
   Using the same formula:
   \[
   A_{fv}' = \frac{A_{vf}'}{1 + m_v} = \frac{111}{1 + 0.1} = \frac{111}{1.1} = 101.0 \text{ V/V}
   \]

4. **Calculate the percentage change in the closed-loop gain:**
   \[
   \text{Percentage Change} = \frac{A_{fv}' - A_{fv}}{A_{fv}} \times 100\% = \frac{101.0 - 90.9}{90.9} \times 100\% = 11.1 \text{ V/V} \times 100\% = 12.1 \text{ V/V}
   \]

**Final Answer:**  
The percentage change in the closed-loop gain is \(\boxed{12.1\%}\).
