Use momentum conservation to calculate the approximate recoil velocity of the Earth when a cannonball is fired. The cannonball has a velocity of 90.0 m/s and a mass of approximately 1 kg. The Earth's mass is \(5.97 \times 10^{24}\) kg.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the recoil velocity of the Earth, we use the conservation of momentum. The total momentum before the explosion (or in this case, the explosion of the cannonball) is zero, as both the cannonball and the Earth are initially at rest. After the explosion, the cannonball has a velocity \(v_c\) and the Earth has a recoil velocity \(v_e\). The total momentum after the explosion must also be zero.

\[
\text{Momentum} = m \times v
\]

\[
m_c \cdot v_c + m_e \cdot v_e = 0
\]

Solving for the recoil velocity \(v_e\):

\[
v_e = -\frac{m_c v_c}{m_e}
\]

Substituting the given values:

\[
v_e = -\frac{(1 \text{ kg})(90.0 \text{ m/s})}{5.97 \times 10^{24} \text{ kg}}
\]

\[
v_e = -1.52 \times 10^{-23} \text{ m/s}
\]

The recoil velocity of the Earth is \(\boxed{-1.52 \times 10^{-23} \text{ m/s}}\). The negative sign indicates that the Earth moves in the opposite direction of the cannonball.
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

