","The intensity of thermal radiation of a black body as a function of temperature \( T \) and wavelength \( \lambda \) is given by the Planck's law formula:

\[
I(\lambda, T) = \frac{\sigma}{4\pi c^2} \left( \frac{\lambda}{T} \right)^{-1} e^{-\left( \frac{\hbar \nu}{kT} \right)}
\]

Here:
- \( I(\lambda, T) \) is the intensity of thermal radiation at wavelength \( \lambda \) and temperature \( T \).
- \( \sigma \) is the Stefan-Boltzmann constant, \( \sigma = 5.67 \times 10^{-8} \, \text{W/m}^2 \cdot \text{K}^4 \).
- \( c \) is the speed of light, \( c = 3 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s} \).
- \( \lambda \) is the wavelength of the radiation.
- \( T \) is the absolute temperature of the black body (in Kelvin).
- \( \hbar \) is the reduced Planck constant, \( \hbar = \frac{h}{2\pi} \approx 1.087 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J s} \), where \( h \) is the Planck constant.
- \( \nu \) is the frequency of the radiation, related to \( \lambda \) by \( \nu = \frac{c}{\lambda} \).
- \( k \) is the Boltzmann constant, \( k = 1.38 \times 10^{-23} \, \text{J K}^{-1} \).

This equation shows the relationship between the intensity of thermal radiation, wavelength, and temperature of a black body.",fluency,"# Task Description
