To verify this, we start with the definition of the \(S\)-matrix:
\[
S_{mn} = \langle n \rangle \langle m \rangle,
\]
where \(\langle n \rangle\) and \(\langle m \rangle\) denote the normalized wave functions of the \(n\)-th and \(m\rangle\)-th states, respectively.

Substituting this into the Wightius-Winger-Winger (WWW) relation:
\[
\sum_l S_{lm} S_{ln} = \sum_l \langle n \rangle \langle m \rangle \langle n \rangle \langle m \rangle = \sum_l \langle n \rangle \langle n \rangle \langle m \rangle \langle m \rangle.
\]
